  "party_positions": [
    {
      "claims": [
        {
          "attribution": "Legislative leaders",
          "citations": [
            {
              "document_hash": "86792f4b70a745703817d47d780cf8aa708a61eca0fa41b27c3df9f5dbc6584b",
              "evidence_ids": [
                "ev-86792f4b70a7-0000-0002"
              ],
              "pages": {
                "end": 2,
                "start": 2
              }
            }
          ],
          "text": "According to Legislative leaders, they argue that without their participation, important state interests would not be adequately represented, especially given the Governor\u2019s opposition to S.B.\u202f824."
        }
      ],
      "heading": "Petitioners (Legislative Leaders)",
      "status": "complete",
      "summary": null,
      "summary_citations": []
    },
    {
      "claims": [
        {
          "attribution": "NAACP",
          "citations": [
            {
              "document_hash": "86792f4b70a745703817d47d780cf8aa708a61eca0fa41b27c3df9f5dbc6584b",
              "evidence_ids": [
                "ev-86792f4b70a7-0001-0005"
              ],
              "pages": {
                "end": 17,
                "start": 17
              }
            }
          ],
          "text": "According to NAACP, they argue that Rule\u202f24(a)(2) permits intervention only by \u201cnew\u201d parties, and the legislative leaders are already \u201cexisting\u201d parties, so intervention should be denied."
        },
        {
          "attribution": "NAACP",
          "citations": [
            {
              "document_hash": "86792f4b70a745703817d47d780cf8aa708a61eca0fa41b27c3df9f5dbc6584b",
              "evidence_ids": [
                "ev-86792f4b70a7-0002-0000"
              ],
              "pages": {
                "end": 22,
                "start": 22
              }
            }
          ],
          "text": "According to NAACP, they warn that allowing the legislative leaders to intervene could make trial management impossible."
        }
      ],
      "heading": "NAACP",
      "status": "complete",
      "summary": null,
      "summary_citations": []
    },
    {
      "claims": [
        {
          "attribution": "David H. Thompson",
          "citations": [
            {
              "document_hash": "79fc0b1b8362518878448abb22147526f16b00a8666702c08abdeb6016459daa",
              "evidence_ids": [
                "ev-79fc0b1b8362-0000-0001"
              ],
              "pages": {
                "end": 5,
                "start": 5
              }
            }
          ],
          "text": "According to David H. Thompson, north Carolina law designates the state as a necessary party and the petitioners as agents of the state in actions challenging state statutes; they were denied intervention in federal court."
        },
        {
          "attribution": "David H. Thompson",
          "citations": [
            {
              "document_hash": "79fc0b1b8362518878448abb22147526f16b00a8666702c08abdeb6016459daa",
              "evidence_ids": [
                "ev-79fc0b1b8362-0000-0004"
              ],
              "pages": {
                "end": 16,
                "start": 16
              }
            }
          ],
          "text": "According to David H. Thompson, federal Rule of Civil Procedure\u202f24(b)(2) reflects the interest in administering a law, not created by state law."
        },
        {
          "attribution": "MR. THOMPSON",
          "citations": [
            {
              "document_hash": "79fc0b1b8362518878448abb22147526f16b00a8666702c08abdeb6016459daa",
              "evidence_ids": [
                "ev-79fc0b1b8362-0001-0001"
              ],
              "pages": {
                "end": 25,
                "start": 24
              }
            }
          ],
          "text": "According to MR. THOMPSON, states can create interests, as shown by Rule\u202f24\u2019s discussion of property and contracts, which are traditionally matters of state law."
        }
      ],
